# Adjective-like
である attaches a predicate of a clause to a noun. Or it may be used predicatively for a formal tone. な may replace it if the "combined picture" is existential in nature. 「データが安全でないことが原因な場合がある。」 — "There are cases in which the cause is that the data is not safe."

の and である may generate an attribute of unessential information. The "main" particle may be replaced with a comma. 「ゼレンスキー大統領は**ロシア軍から奪還した黒海の拠点の島**、ズミイヌイ島を訪れた。」 — "President Zelensky visited Zmiinyi Island, **an island base in the Black Sea which \[they\] recaptured from the Russian Army**."

<!-- is the continuative form essentially nominalization? -->
When needing to create an attribute with a verb at the end of a clause, turn it into its continuative form then follow it with の. 「**土砂降り**の雨」 — "**Pouring** rain." Though, using the attributive form of the verb is making a comeback.